{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons (often by ~3–10 EUR), especially during strong uptrends; apply an upward level adjustment when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model underestimates the slope during rallies (fails to capture rapid step-ups); add a momentum term (e.g., proportional to last 5–10d return) that increases with horizon (bigger uplift for 10–25d).",
    "Premature mean reversion: forecasts revert toward ~72–76 even when the market sustains ~78–84; delay mean-reversion forces unless there is clear downside confirmation (e.g., multiple consecutive down closes).",
    "Horizon-specific bias: mid-to-long horizons (≈10–25d) show the largest underprediction; use a horizon-dependent uplift (small for 1–5d, larger for 10–25d, tapering slightly by day 30).",
    "Downturn mis-timing: when the model predicts late-horizon declines, it often turns down too early and too far; shift any projected peak/rollover later (right-shift the turning point) and reduce the magnitude of late-horizon drawdowns unless recent realized volatility is rising on the downside.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized swings; widen forecast dispersion (or allow larger day-to-day increments) during trending regimes to avoid systematic underestimation of highs."
  ]
}
